# Push targets and PR safety

This repository has `origin` configured as the fork `jgrace19/excalidraw`. Workshop work, kit changes, and any feature work must target this fork. Do not push to the upstream `excalidraw/excalidraw` repository, and do not push directly to a default branch on any remote.

## Required

- Push branches only to `origin` (the `jgrace19/excalidraw` fork). Verify with `git remote -v` before pushing if you are not sure.
- Open all PRs against `jgrace19/excalidraw`'s default branch — not against upstream `excalidraw/excalidraw`.
- Never push directly to `main`, `master`, or any default branch on any remote. Always use a feature branch.

## Verifying before pushing

Run the following before any push:

```
git remote -v
git branch --show-current
```

- The remote URL should be `https://github.com/jgrace19/excalidraw.git` (or the SSH equivalent).
- The current branch should not be `main` or `master`.

If a second remote (e.g. an `upstream` pointing at `excalidraw/excalidraw`) is ever added, push explicitly with the remote name to avoid ambiguity:

```
git push origin <branch-name>
```

## Opening a PR

Use the GitHub UI or `gh pr create` with an explicit `--repo` flag:

```
gh pr create --repo jgrace19/excalidraw
```

Without `--repo`, `gh` may infer the wrong target — particularly if an upstream remote is configured.

## If you have committed directly on a default branch

Do not push. Move the commit to a feature branch first:

```
git switch -c <feature-branch>
git switch main
git reset --hard origin/main
git switch <feature-branch>
git push -u origin <feature-branch>
```

Then open a PR.

## If you have already pushed somewhere wrong

- Pushed to upstream `excalidraw/excalidraw`: stop and tell the user. Do not force-push or delete the remote branch without explicit confirmation.
- Pushed to a default branch on the fork: stop and tell the user. Offer to revert via a follow-up PR.

## Exception

Only push directly to a default branch, or to upstream, when the user explicitly authorizes that specific operation. A generic "commit and push" never grants this permission — interpret it as "commit on a feature branch and open a PR against the fork."
